WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.460
<v ->Okay, so now let's discuss CloudTrail</v>

2
00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.280
and some solution architectures that can be interesting

3
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.600
with CloudTrail.

4
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.430
So the first one is around

5
00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:10.920
how to deliver files from CloudTrail to S3

6
00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.100
and the type of use case that it opens up.

7
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.180
So we have CloudTrail, and you can set up a dump

8
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.950
of the CloudTrail files into S3 every five minutes.

9
00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:24.990
And so this encryption can be enabled.

10
00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.110
And by default it's going to be SSE-S3 encryption.

11
00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.020
But you can set up manually SSE-KMS

12
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.060
for your CloudTrail files.

13
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.360
You could set up a lifecycle policy on your S3 buckets

14
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.540
to deliver files to the Glacier tier

15
00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:40.680
in case they're archives

16
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.220
and you don't need to have them around for a long time

17
00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.570
or you just wanna archive them

18
00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.820
and make sure that if you need to access them

19
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.220
you can wait maybe for 6, 12 hours

20
00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:51.633
before you analyze these.

21
00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:55.050
When a file is delivered into S3,

22
00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:59.310
you can leverage S3 events to notify either an SQS queue,

23
00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:02.070
an SNS topic, or a Lambda function.

24
00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.680
But you can also have CloudTrail deliver notifications

25
00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.270
directly into SNS.

26
00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:10.380
And from SNS you could invoke SQS or Lambda.

27
00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.810
So this gives you a bunch of different options

28
00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.640
you can have for this architecture.

29
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.830
As you can see, there's not one way of doing things.

30
00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.870
But getting into thinking

31
00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.930
how these things fit together is the key

32
00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:25.020
to passing the Solutions Architect Professional exam.

33
00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.940
Okay, on top of it, you need to think

34
00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.730
about all the enhancements we can have from S3.

35
00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.670
So we can enable versioning to prevent accidental deletes.

36
00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.950
We can have MFA for delete protection.

37
00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.630
We can have S3 lifecycle policies, as I said,

38
00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:39.120
to move it to S3 IA or Glacier.

39
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.310
You can have S3 object lock to make sure

40
00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:44.100
that the S3 buckets' objects are never deleted

41
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.930
and will never be modified as well.

42
00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.370
So this could be very powerful.

43
00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:51.450
You can enable encryption such as SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS.

44
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.070
And you can even have a feature

45
00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:56.190
to perform CloudTrail log file integrity validation

46
00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.020
that will verify that the files delivered

47
00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.630
in S3 are exactly as they should be

48
00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.220
and they have not been tempered with,

49
00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:03.750
they have not been modified.

50
00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.520
So this is getting interesting, right?

51
00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.043
Because from CloudTrail, in combining this

52
00:02:08.043 --> 00:02:09.780
with S3 and all these notifications,

53
00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.270
we can have a bunch of use case being opened up.

54
00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:15.570
So this is hopefully opening some stuff for you

55
00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.180
and helping you understand the kind of architectures

56
00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.970
we can be looking at going into the exam.

57
00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.920
Okay, another architecture now.

58
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.410
CloudTrail can be multi-account and multi-region.

59
00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.500
So say we have two accounts, Account A and Account B.

60
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.180
And we have a security account

61
00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.190
that we want to send the logs to.

62
00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.690
So we'll have CloudTrail in the first account

63
00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.820
and CloudTrail in the second account.

64
00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.820
And we'll set up an S3 bucket that will be holding the logs

65
00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.620
of all these CloudTrails.

66
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.020
The thing is CloudTrail needs to be delivering

67
00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:44.940
these log files into the S3 buckets

68
00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.560
of the security account.

69
00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.870
And so the only way for us to do it is

70
00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.790
to define an S3 bucket policy.

71
00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.790
And the S3 bucket policy should allow CloudTrail

72
00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.130
to deliver files into S3.

73
00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:57.840
And the cool thing we have here is

74
00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.600
that the S3 bucket policy, first of all, is necessary

75
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.480
and very simple to maintain for cross-account delivery.

76
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.560
The second is that if Account A needs to access

77
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.530
you know, the logs for the CloudTrail bucket

78
00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.020
we can create, for example, a cross-account role

79
00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.420
and assume the role in the security account.

80
00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.600
Or we can edit the bucket policy to allow reads

81
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.010
from the Account A.

82
00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.270
So it's really up to you to see

83
00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.010
the kind of architectures then you need.

84
00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.080
But it's really interesting here to see

85
00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.210
that there's different options to provide the reads access

86
00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:29.070
to S3 based on the use case.

87
00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:30.690
And on top of it, we have demonstrated

88
00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:33.150
a good way to have a security account in the middle

89
00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.090
to keep all these CloudTrail logs into a secure place.

90
00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:38.340
And maybe the security account has a much tighter security

91
00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.020
for user management and so on.

92
00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.060
And so we have the certainty that these logs are safe

93
00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.160
and secure for a long time.

94
00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.710
Okay, so yet another solution architecture.

95
00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:48.060
Say you want to create alerts

96
00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.220
when certain API calls are done.

97
00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.510
So you have CloudTrail.

98
00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:55.050
And you can stream all these events into CloudWatch Logs.

99
00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:56.670
And then from CloudWatch Logs you can open up

100
00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:57.780
a bunch of use case.

101
00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.000
And one of the things you can do with CloudWatch logs is

102
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.800
to create a metrics filter

103
00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.660
and then a CloudWatch alarm on top of it.

104
00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.580
And this filter will basically filter

105
00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.070
for the API call you're looking for.

106
00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:10.470
Maybe you're looking for a terminate instance.

107
00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.260
So you create metric filter for terminate instance.

108
00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.720
And then if that filter passes through

109
00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.897
then you would have the metric incremented by one.

110
00:04:18.897 --> 00:04:21.360
And maybe your CloudWatch alarm is set up to be triggered

111
00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.820
whenever the metric filter is equals to one,

112
00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.700
in which case the CloudWatch alarm can deliver

113
00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:27.930
to an SNS topic.

114
00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.450
And from that SNS topic, you could have a Lambda function.

115
00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:31.500
You could have an SQS queue.

116
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:32.850
You could have multiple things.

117
00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:35.703
But this would be a way to create alerts for API calls.

118
00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.420
Now these log metric filters can be detecting also

119
00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:40.890
a high level of API happening.

120
00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:43.080
Doesn't have to be a specific API.

121
00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.810
So you can be counting the occurrences of a specific API,

122
00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.030
for example terminate EC2 instances

123
00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.580
or count the API calls per user

124
00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.040
or detect a high level of denied API calls.

125
00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.010
Maybe someone is trying to breach into your security.

126
00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:57.570
So it's not just for one API call

127
00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.520
you can be creating a metrics filter.

128
00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:01.650
And CloudWatch alarms is for counting them

129
00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:06.000
or detecting high level of unusual activity.

130
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:07.410
Okay, so there is another feature

131
00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:08.340
of CloudTrail need to know about,

132
00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.650
which is the organizational trail.

133
00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:13.140
So if you have AWS organizations,

134
00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:14.850
for example, you have a management account,

135
00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.400
and then you have several OUs with other member accounts,

136
00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.650
then it is possible for you to set up

137
00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:23.610
a organizational trail directly in the management accounts.

138
00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:25.200
Okay, so it's very important for you to remember

139
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.720
this trail is created in the management accounts

140
00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:29.670
and not in the children accounts,

141
00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:32.250
in the member accounts of your organization.

142
00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:34.440
Now what's happening is that once you create this trail

143
00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:37.980
then any events into all the member accounts will be

144
00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:39.960
monitored by this trail directly,

145
00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:41.550
which is very easy to understand.

146
00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.530
So that means that any accounts in the prod OU

147
00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:45.510
or the dev OU will be monitored.

148
00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:48.630
And from there, while you can send the information

149
00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:51.420
from a trail into an S3 bucket, that will be again

150
00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:52.740
in your management accounts.

151
00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:54.600
And this S3 bucket will contain

152
00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.790
a certain kind of organization,

153
00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:58.950
where as you see the suffix

154
00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:02.430
of the S3 prefix represents the account number

155
00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.923
that is being monitored by CloudTrail.

156
00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:09.750
So how can you react to events the fastest in CloudTrail?

157
00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:12.570
Well, first of all, any event in CloudTrail may take

158
00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:14.340
15 minutes to be delivered.

159
00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:15.540
So this is something to know.

160
00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.270
But anyways, so we have EventBridge.

161
00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:20.640
And this is cool because this can be triggered

162
00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:23.730
for any API call done within CloudTrail,

163
00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:26.430
which gives you by far the fastest, most reactive way

164
00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.980
to react to any events in CloudTrail.

165
00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:31.710
So you can have deep integration between them.

166
00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:34.200
Then we have CloudTrail Delivery in CloudWatch logs.

167
00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.210
So that means that all the events are going to be streamed

168
00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:37.380
in the CloudWatch Logs.

169
00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.750
And you can create a metric filter

170
00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.240
to analyze occurrences and detect anomalies.

171
00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:43.860
So this is when you wanna see

172
00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:47.070
if someone is trying too fast to log into your accounts,

173
00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:49.110
that kind of things that could be a trigger

174
00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:50.940
for maybe an attack.

175
00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.760
And you have CloudTrail Delivery in Amazon S3.

176
00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.700
So there the events are delivered every five minutes.

177
00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.740
And you have also the possibility to guarantee

178
00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:02.070
that the logs in S3 are having integrity

179
00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:03.960
with a hashing feature.

180
00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:05.580
You can deliver them cross-account.

181
00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:08.700
It's good for long-term storage and analytics.

182
00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:10.080
So based on use case you have,

183
00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.150
if you want to react to an event right away,

184
00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:13.140
it's EventBridge.

185
00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.060
If you want to count events,

186
00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:16.470
it's going to be CloudWatch Logs.

187
00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:19.320
And if you want to do analytics on all of them at scale,

188
00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:21.420
it's going to be Amazon S3.

189
00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:22.500
So that's it for this lecture.

190
00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:23.580
I hope you liked it.

191
00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:25.530
And I will see you in the next lecture.

